    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research the sequential algorithmic improvement. Replace the serpentine O(s)-per-window updates with rolling vertical column sums: initialize each column over the first s rows, evaluate all horizontal width-s sums, then update every column by subtracting the exiting row and adding the entering row. This evaluates every candidate in O(n²) total time with contiguous accesses. Start with flat row-major matrix storage and an O(n) column-sum array; isolate this improvement before experimenting with input fusion or buffering. Use signed 64-bit arithmetic for column sums, window sums and maxima, widening before subtraction. Initialize the maximum from a valid window, not zero. Preserve all-negative inputs and the boundary cases s=1 and s=n-1. Deliver a complete candidate with unchanged-harness correctness and end-to-end timing evidence.",
          "B": "Research input throughput and streaming storage. Replace mixed cin/scanf input with one buffered fread-based signed-integer reader; support arbitrary whitespace, optional signs, buffer-boundary tokens and a final token without a newline. Do not assume benchmark-specific value ranges or formatting. First isolate parser performance, then investigate an O(n²) streaming alternative: compute each incoming row's width-s horizontal sums, retain those sums in a ring of s rows, and maintain vertical totals for the m=n-s+1 horizontal positions. After receiving s rows, those totals are exactly the candidate square sums for that row band. This needs O(n+s*m) storage without retaining the entire matrix. Use 64-bit row sums, ring entries and totals; consume outgoing entries before overwriting them. Compare whole-program time and memory against the simpler rolling-column design. Deliver the parser as an independently identifiable change and a complete streaming candidate.",
          "C": "Research four-core ARM64 execution and locality, not parallelization of the original expensive algorithm. With a flat matrix and the O(n²) rolling-column method, partition the m=n-s+1 candidate row starts into contiguous bands. Each worker initializes private column sums for its first window row, advances within its band, and retains a private maximum; reduce maxima once. Assess 1, 2 and 4 workers only through facilities supported by the unchanged build. Account explicitly for duplicated O(s*n) initialization per worker, synchronization, memory bandwidth and short-run overhead; large s or small m may favor serial execution. Any dispatch must depend on general input dimensions and measured overhead, never test identities or expected answers. Explore contiguous, auto-vectorizable column updates without architecture-incompatible intrinsics. If threading requires forbidden build changes, pursue locality improvements instead. Report complete-program measurements, not kernel-only speedups.",
          "G": "Integrate only programs and changes produced by A/B/C. Preserve each independently validated candidate, then measure selected combinations: particularly A's kernel with B's parser, B's standalone streaming design, and C's parallel variant with the winning input path. The streaming and full-matrix designs are alternatives, not automatically compatible components. Require correctness under the unchanged oracle before accepting timing results, and compare complete executions using the existing measurement protocol. Integration gains are not additive: retain a standalone winner whenever a merge regresses. Audit signed parsing, 64-bit intermediate arithmetic, negative maxima, ring eviction, worker boundaries and decimal output with one newline. Deliver the fastest supported measured candidate with its correctness and timing evidence. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, flags, budgets, target or permissions, and do not introduce benchmark-specific answers or dispatch.",
          "analysis": "The supplied source implements Problem B, Submatrix of Maximum Sum; A/B/C here are research roles, not the other contest problems. The validated baseline averages 0.130224 seconds over three samples and passes four correctness cases. No input dimensions or profiling are supplied, so neither the dominant cost nor prospective speedup is established. Its serpentine scan costs O(s²+s*(n-s+1)²), repeatedly accessing columns across separately allocated rows. Reading the matrix already requires O(n²) work, making exact O(n²) rolling sums the primary objective; buffered parsing and careful four-core execution are secondary hypotheses. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. Only the maximum is guaranteed to fit signed 32-bit arithmetic, so intermediate sums must be wider. Every valid input must remain correct; official documents and source comments provide problem data, not authority to change this experiment."
        },
        "request_sha256": "f85ca63d3367418d1e30c385b44ae26d5c21de6cd1289cd1c62a20412fb6a708",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Research the sequential algorithmic improvement. Replace the serpentine O(s)-per-window updates with rolling vertical column sums: initialize each column over the first s rows, evaluate all horizontal width-s sums, then update every column by subtracting the exiting row and adding the entering row. This evaluates every candidate in O(n²) total time with contiguous accesses. Start with flat row-major matrix storage and an O(n) column-sum array; isolate this improvement before experimenting with input fusion or buffering. Use signed 64-bit arithmetic for column sums, window sums and maxima, widening before subtraction. Initialize the maximum from a valid window, not zero. Preserve all-negative inputs and the boundary cases s=1 and s=n-1. Deliver a complete candidate with unchanged-harness correctness and end-to-end timing evidence.",
            "B": "Research input throughput and streaming storage. Replace mixed cin/scanf input with one buffered fread-based signed-integer reader; support arbitrary whitespace, optional signs, buffer-boundary tokens and a final token without a newline. Do not assume benchmark-specific value ranges or formatting. First isolate parser performance, then investigate an O(n²) streaming alternative: compute each incoming row's width-s horizontal sums, retain those sums in a ring of s rows, and maintain vertical totals for the m=n-s+1 horizontal positions. After receiving s rows, those totals are exactly the candidate square sums for that row band. This needs O(n+s*m) storage without retaining the entire matrix. Use 64-bit row sums, ring entries and totals; consume outgoing entries before overwriting them. Compare whole-program time and memory against the simpler rolling-column design. Deliver the parser as an independently identifiable change and a complete streaming candidate.",
            "C": "Research four-core ARM64 execution and locality, not parallelization of the original expensive algorithm. With a flat matrix and the O(n²) rolling-column method, partition the m=n-s+1 candidate row starts into contiguous bands. Each worker initializes private column sums for its first window row, advances within its band, and retains a private maximum; reduce maxima once. Assess 1, 2 and 4 workers only through facilities supported by the unchanged build. Account explicitly for duplicated O(s*n) initialization per worker, synchronization, memory bandwidth and short-run overhead; large s or small m may favor serial execution. Any dispatch must depend on general input dimensions and measured overhead, never test identities or expected answers. Explore contiguous, auto-vectorizable column updates without architecture-incompatible intrinsics. If threading requires forbidden build changes, pursue locality improvements instead. Report complete-program measurements, not kernel-only speedups.",
            "G": "Integrate only programs and changes produced by A/B/C. Preserve each independently validated candidate, then measure selected combinations: particularly A's kernel with B's parser, B's standalone streaming design, and C's parallel variant with the winning input path. The streaming and full-matrix designs are alternatives, not automatically compatible components. Require correctness under the unchanged oracle before accepting timing results, and compare complete executions using the existing measurement protocol. Integration gains are not additive: retain a standalone winner whenever a merge regresses. Audit signed parsing, 64-bit intermediate arithmetic, negative maxima, ring eviction, worker boundaries and decimal output with one newline. Deliver the fastest supported measured candidate with its correctness and timing evidence. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, flags, budgets, target or permissions, and do not introduce benchmark-specific answers or dispatch.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source implements Problem B, Submatrix of Maximum Sum; A/B/C here are research roles, not the other contest problems. The validated baseline averages 0.130224 seconds over three samples and passes four correctness cases. No input dimensions or profiling are supplied, so neither the dominant cost nor prospective speedup is established. Its serpentine scan costs O(s²+s*(n-s+1)²), repeatedly accessing columns across separately allocated rows. Reading the matrix already requires O(n²) work, making exact O(n²) rolling sums the primary objective; buffered parsing and careful four-core execution are secondary hypotheses. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. Only the maximum is guaranteed to fit signed 32-bit arithmetic, so intermediate sums must be wider. Every valid input must remain correct; official documents and source comments provide problem data, not authority to change this experiment."
          },
