    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research compact characteristic-polynomial exponentiation as the primary replacement for dense matrices. Define Q(x)=x^N-P1*x^(N-1)-...-PN. Compute r=x^(H-1) modulo Q and return sum(r[i]*A[i+1]) modulo 1000000007. Reduce a coefficient at degree d>=N by distributing it to degrees d-j with multiplier Pj, processing d downward. Target O(N^2 log H) time and O(N) working storage. Investigate left-to-right exponentiation: square/reduce each bit, then multiply by x in O(N) for set bits, avoiding a second general polynomial multiplication. Reuse buffers and benchmark symmetry-aware squaring. Handle N=1 correctly, where x modulo Q is P1. Establish coefficient orientation and overflow safety before optimizing; do not parallelize dependent reduction degrees naively. Deliver a correct standalone implementation and measurements under the unchanged harness.",
          "B": "Independently research Bostan-Mori coefficient extraction. Let V(x)=1-P1*x-...-PN*x^N and U(x)=[(A1+A2*x+...+AN*x^(N-1))*V(x)] modulo x^N; seek coefficient H-1 of U/V. At each iteration form U*V(-x) and V*V(-x), retain numerator coefficients matching the current index parity and denominator even coefficients, then halve the index. Finish with U(0)/V(0); V(0) remains 1 here. Start with compact quadratic convolutions, exploiting parity and denominator symmetry. Compare against A rather than assuming better constants. Only investigate exact multi-prime NTT/CRT convolution if measured large-N benefits justify its setup cost; 1000000007 cannot directly support the needed radix-2 transforms. Any CRT approach must reconstruct convolution coefficients with a proven sufficient modulus product before reduction to the answer modulus. Avoid unproved floating-point convolution. Deliver independent correctness reasoning and measured standalone results.",
          "C": "Research low-overhead short-horizon execution and ARM64 arithmetic. Implement direct recurrence evaluation using an N-element circular buffer and correctly ordered coefficients, costing O((H-N)*s), where s is the number of nonzero recurrence coefficients. Bound this route with an operation-count-based crossover and retain a correct logarithmic-horizon fallback; never iterate toward H=10^9 indiscriminately. Skipping genuinely zero coefficients is valid, but do not identify benchmark inputs or cache answers. Investigate reusable 32-bit residue buffers, 64-bit products, and delayed modular reductions with explicit accumulator bounds: an entire N-term dot product does not fit uint64_t in general. Measure serial execution first. Explore up to four workers only where independent work is sufficient and the unchanged build supports threading; avoid per-inner-loop scheduling and oversubscription. Supply a standalone hybrid and clearly separable kernels or dispatch logic for G.",
          "G": "Integrate only programs actually produced by A/B/C. First compare their standalone correctness and end-to-end timings against the retained baseline. Then evaluate justified combinations, especially C's bounded direct-recurrence path with either A's polynomial engine or B's coefficient-extraction engine. Measure every integrated candidate: arithmetic, dispatch, allocation and threading changes can interact negatively, so gains are not additive. Select crossovers from general operation counts and permitted measurements, using dimensions, horizon and genuine sparsity—not test identities or expected outputs. Require reasoning for N=1, H=N+1, zero coefficients/base values, maximum residues, recurrence orientation and accumulator overflow in addition to passing the unchanged oracle. Preserve the exact stdin/stdout contract and all compiler, target, test, oracle, permission and budget settings. Keep the best validated artifact and report timings and uncertainty without claiming unmeasured scalability.",
          "analysis": "The supplied source targets NPbonacci, not the other problems in the statement. It computes one recurrence term through dense companion-matrix exponentiation, costing O(N^3 log(H-N)), with modular reduction in every inner product, large fixed buffers and unnecessary full-matrix results. Replacing that representation is the strongest opportunity for N<=1020 and H<=10^9. This run establishes only a correct baseline on four cases: mean 0.020409 seconds, with samples spanning approximately 0.020249–0.020596 seconds. Input dimensions and profiling are unavailable; this does not establish a timing floor or a parallelism benefit. Accordingly, A and B explore independent exact quadratic-time recurrence algorithms, while C targets short horizons and implementation overhead. The four-core ARM64 target favors compact CPU implementations; NTT and threading must earn their overhead through actual measurements. All routes must remain correct for every valid input."
        },
        "request_sha256": "69577198c9922eaec8d46e026530324c5c380993980fe42b9c88a7d8c520c7a6",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Research compact characteristic-polynomial exponentiation as the primary replacement for dense matrices. Define Q(x)=x^N-P1*x^(N-1)-...-PN. Compute r=x^(H-1) modulo Q and return sum(r[i]*A[i+1]) modulo 1000000007. Reduce a coefficient at degree d>=N by distributing it to degrees d-j with multiplier Pj, processing d downward. Target O(N^2 log H) time and O(N) working storage. Investigate left-to-right exponentiation: square/reduce each bit, then multiply by x in O(N) for set bits, avoiding a second general polynomial multiplication. Reuse buffers and benchmark symmetry-aware squaring. Handle N=1 correctly, where x modulo Q is P1. Establish coefficient orientation and overflow safety before optimizing; do not parallelize dependent reduction degrees naively. Deliver a correct standalone implementation and measurements under the unchanged harness.",
            "B": "Independently research Bostan-Mori coefficient extraction. Let V(x)=1-P1*x-...-PN*x^N and U(x)=[(A1+A2*x+...+AN*x^(N-1))*V(x)] modulo x^N; seek coefficient H-1 of U/V. At each iteration form U*V(-x) and V*V(-x), retain numerator coefficients matching the current index parity and denominator even coefficients, then halve the index. Finish with U(0)/V(0); V(0) remains 1 here. Start with compact quadratic convolutions, exploiting parity and denominator symmetry. Compare against A rather than assuming better constants. Only investigate exact multi-prime NTT/CRT convolution if measured large-N benefits justify its setup cost; 1000000007 cannot directly support the needed radix-2 transforms. Any CRT approach must reconstruct convolution coefficients with a proven sufficient modulus product before reduction to the answer modulus. Avoid unproved floating-point convolution. Deliver independent correctness reasoning and measured standalone results.",
            "C": "Research low-overhead short-horizon execution and ARM64 arithmetic. Implement direct recurrence evaluation using an N-element circular buffer and correctly ordered coefficients, costing O((H-N)*s), where s is the number of nonzero recurrence coefficients. Bound this route with an operation-count-based crossover and retain a correct logarithmic-horizon fallback; never iterate toward H=10^9 indiscriminately. Skipping genuinely zero coefficients is valid, but do not identify benchmark inputs or cache answers. Investigate reusable 32-bit residue buffers, 64-bit products, and delayed modular reductions with explicit accumulator bounds: an entire N-term dot product does not fit uint64_t in general. Measure serial execution first. Explore up to four workers only where independent work is sufficient and the unchanged build supports threading; avoid per-inner-loop scheduling and oversubscription. Supply a standalone hybrid and clearly separable kernels or dispatch logic for G.",
            "G": "Integrate only programs actually produced by A/B/C. First compare their standalone correctness and end-to-end timings against the retained baseline. Then evaluate justified combinations, especially C's bounded direct-recurrence path with either A's polynomial engine or B's coefficient-extraction engine. Measure every integrated candidate: arithmetic, dispatch, allocation and threading changes can interact negatively, so gains are not additive. Select crossovers from general operation counts and permitted measurements, using dimensions, horizon and genuine sparsity—not test identities or expected outputs. Require reasoning for N=1, H=N+1, zero coefficients/base values, maximum residues, recurrence orientation and accumulator overflow in addition to passing the unchanged oracle. Preserve the exact stdin/stdout contract and all compiler, target, test, oracle, permission and budget settings. Keep the best validated artifact and report timings and uncertainty without claiming unmeasured scalability.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source targets NPbonacci, not the other problems in the statement. It computes one recurrence term through dense companion-matrix exponentiation, costing O(N^3 log(H-N)), with modular reduction in every inner product, large fixed buffers and unnecessary full-matrix results. Replacing that representation is the strongest opportunity for N<=1020 and H<=10^9. This run establishes only a correct baseline on four cases: mean 0.020409 seconds, with samples spanning approximately 0.020249–0.020596 seconds. Input dimensions and profiling are unavailable; this does not establish a timing floor or a parallelism benefit. Accordingly, A and B explore independent exact quadratic-time recurrence algorithms, while C targets short horizons and implementation overhead. The four-core ARM64 target favors compact CPU implementations; NTT and threading must earn their overhead through actual measurements. All routes must remain correct for every valid input."
          },
